        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q51.png",
        "diagram": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/diagram-51.png"
      },
      {
        "number": 52,
        "page": 11,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "A bag contains 50 metallic spheres of different diameters. A task is given to students to\nclassify these spheres in terms of their diameters and find the number of spheres that lie\nbetween the range of 0.3 cm and 1.5 cm out of 50 metallic spheres.\nFor classifying as accurately as possible, the MOST appropriate instrument the students\nshould pick from the science lab is",
        "choices": {
          "A": "spherometre.",
          "B": "screw gauge.",
          "C": "measuring tape.",
          "D": "Vernier callipers."
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q52.png",
        "diagram": null
      },
      {
        "number": 53,
        "page": 11,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "The given image shows a measuring instrument.\nOn the main scale of this instrument, the purpose of markings above and below the zero mark\nis to measure the\nI.\ndepth.\nII.\nheight.\nIII.\nthickness.",
        "choices": {
          "A": "I only",
          "B": "III only",
          "C": "I and II",
          "D": "II and III"
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q53.png",
        "diagram": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q53.png"
      },
      {
        "number": 54,
        "page": 12,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "The given diagram shows a helical spring attached to a fixed surface. The value of spring\nconstant is taken as ‘k’ 300 N/m and a 100 N weight is attached at the bottom.\nThe extension in the spring will be",
        "choices": {
          "A": "0.333 m",
          "B": "0.666 m",
          "C": "3 m",
          "D": "6 m"
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q54.png",
        "diagram": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q54.png"
      },
      {
        "number": 55,
        "page": 12,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "Which of the given sources of errors will NOT affect your readings while handling the\nGravesand’s apparatus?",
        "choices": {
          "A": "Friction in the pulleys",
          "B": "Use of rusty holding pins",
          "C": "Use of inaccurate weights",
          "D": "Placement of the drawing board"
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q55.png",
        "diagram": null
      },
      {
        "number": 56,
        "page": 12,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "The given diagram shows a ticker tape. The frequency of the ticker tape is 20 Hz.\nHow much time will be taken by the ticker tape to plot dots from P to T?",
        "choices": {
          "A": "0.05 s",
          "B": "0.25 s",
          "C": "5 s",
          "D": "20 s"
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q56.png",
        "diagram": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/diagram-56.png"
      },
      {
        "number": 57,
        "page": 13,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "The given figure shows dots on a ticker tape after performing an experiment.\nThe value of acceleration for the bracket region X will be",
        "choices": {
          "A": "$a<0$",
          "B": "$a=0$",
          "C": "$a>0$",
          "D": "$0<a<1$"
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q57.png",
        "diagram": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/diagram-57.png"
      },
      {
        "number": 58,
        "page": 13,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "If a student plans to make a simple pendulum of time period 1 s, then the length\nof the pendulum will be\n(Note: Take the value of acceleration due to gravity as 10 m/s².)",
        "choices": {
          "A": "0.25 m.",
          "B": "0.50 m.",
          "C": "1.00 m.",
          "D": "2.00 m."
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q58.png",
        "diagram": null
      },
      {
        "number": 59,
        "page": 13,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "A second pendulum is oscillating in an elevator.\nIf the elevator falls freely towards the Earth, then the time period of the pendulum will\nbecome",
        "choices": {
          "A": "0 s.",
          "B": "1 s.",
          "C": "2 s.",
          "D": "∞."
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q59.png",
        "diagram": null
      },
      {
        "number": 60,
        "page": 13,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "A student performs an experiment in his college laboratory to determine the specific heat\nof water by electrical method. At the end of experiment, the graph that he drew between\ntemperature (T) and time (t) was incorrect.\nWhich of the following can be a source of error that caused inaccurate reading during his\nobservation?",
        "choices": {
          "A": "Loss of heat due to radiation",
          "B": "Large increase in temperature of water",
          "C": "Greater least count of the thermometer",
          "D": "Non-steady voltage of the power supply"
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q60.png",
        "diagram": null
      },
      {
        "number": 61,
        "page": 14,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "A cylindrical resonance tube, opened at both ends, has a fundamental frequency ‘f’ in air\nas shown in the given figure I.\nAs shown in figure II, if half of the length is dipped vertically into water, then the\nfundamental frequency of the air column will become",
        "choices": {
          "A": "$\\frac f2$",
          "B": "$f$",
          "C": "$\\frac{3f}{2}$",
          "D": "$2f$"
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q61.png",
        "diagram": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/diagram-61.png"
      },
      {
        "number": 62,
        "page": 14,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "The distance between two consecutive nodes of an incident wave is 2 m.\nIf the frequency of the wave is 50 Hz, then the speed of this wave is",
        "choices": {
          "A": "25 m/s.",
          "B": "50 m/s.",
          "C": "100 m/s.",
          "D": "200 m/s."
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q62.png",
        "diagram": null
      },
      {
        "number": 63,
        "page": 14,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "Using the observation table, what electrical energy is consumed in 2 s at 5 V and 2 A?",
        "choices": {
          "A": "5 J.",
          "B": "10 J.",
          "C": "20 J.",
          "D": "40 J."
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q63.png",
        "diagram": null
      },
      {
        "number": 64,
        "page": 14,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "All of the following are precautions which should be taken into consideration while measuring\nthe mechanical equivalent of heat by electric method EXCEPT that the",
        "choices": {
          "A": "supply of voltage is steady.",
          "B": "heating coil should be immersed in water.",
          "C": "calorimeter should be placed in an insulation box.",
          "D": "thermometer should touch the base of the calorimeter."
        },
        "facsimile": "/paper-crops/PHY-XI-2021-P1/q64.png",
        "diagram": null
      },
      {
        "number": 65,
        "page": 15,
        "marks": 1,
        "stem": "A resonance tube is driven at fixed frequency and temperature. The diagram shows successive resonances with the first shown length equal to three quarters of a wavelength. What are the lengths of the next two air columns?",
        "choices": {
          "A": "$L_2=\\frac54\\lambda,\\ L_3=\\frac74\\lambda$",
